# Enhancing Stability and Efficiency of Perovskite Solar Cells with a Bilayer Hole Transporting Layer of Nickel Phthalocyanine and Poly(3‐Hexylthiophene)

## abstract
To expedite the commercialization of perovskite solar cells (PSCs), researchers are exploring the feasibility of employing nickel phthalocyanine (NiPc) as a hole transport material (HTM) due to its cost‐effectiveness, excellent thermal stability, and suitability for solution coating. However, the low LUMO energy level of the NiPc may limit its ability to block photoelectrons generated in the perovskite layer from recombining with holes, which can reduce the overall efficiency of the solar cell. One solution is to use cascaded bilayers with HTMs that have relatively higher LUMO levels. In this study, a bilayer consisting of NiPc and poly(3‐hexylthiophene) (P3HT) is employed as the HTM, where the P3HT exhibits vertical phase separation during the coating process. By optimizing the mixing amount of P3HT into the NiPc, a record power conversion efficiency of 23.11%, the highest reported for NiPc‐based PSCs is achieved. Moreover, an excellent long‐term stability is demonstrated by encapsulating the PSC in polyisobutylene, with the device retaining 90% of its initial efficiency after exposure to 85 °C and 85% relative humidity for 1000 h.

To expedite the commercialization of perovskite solar cells (PSCs), researchers are exploring the feasibility of employing nickel phthalocyanine (NiPc) as a hole transport material (HTM) due to its cost-effectiveness, excellent thermal stability, and suitability for solution coating. However, the low LUMO energy level of the NiPc may limit its ability to block photoelectrons generated in the perovskite layer from recombining with holes, which can reduce the overall efficiency of the solar cell. One solution is to use cascaded bilayers with HTMs that have relatively higher LUMO levels. In this study, a bilayer consisting of NiPc and poly(3-hexylthiophene) (P3HT) is employed as the HTM, where the P3HT exhibits vertical phase separation during the coating process. By optimizing the mixing amount of P3HT into the NiPc, a record power conversion efficiency of $23.17\%$ , the highest reported for NiPc-based PSCs is achieved. Moreover, an excellent long-term stability is demonstrated by encapsulating the PSC in polyisobutylene, with the device retaining $90\%$ of its initial efficiency after exposure to $85^{\circ}C$ and $85\%$ relative humidity for $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{1000}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ .
## 1. Introduction
Inorganic–organic lead halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have shown great potential for high-efficiency and costeffective photovoltaic applications.[1] However, one of the main challenges facing PSCs is their poor stability,[2] which can limit their long-term performance and practical applications. The sensitivity of perovskite materials to moisture and oxygen is one of the primary reasons for the low stability of PSCs.[3] To protect PSCs from moisture and oxygen penetration, encapsulation is often considered the most effective method.[4] In addition, $2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ - tetrakis(N,N-di-p-methoxyphenylamine) $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD), which is commonly used as a hole transport material (HTM) in PSCs, may also contribute to the poor stability of the PSCs.[5] To overcome these limitations, researchers have explored alternative HTMs that have higher chemical stability, such as metal oxides,[6] conjugated polymers,[7] and small molecules.[8] While these materials have demonstrated favorable outcomes in stability and efficiency and have the potential to substitute spiro-OMeTAD in upcoming PSCs, it is important to take into account the ease of processing and the cost of the materials themselves.
Nickel phthalocyanine (NiPc) has been shown to possess excellent hole-transporting properties and high thermal stability,[9,10] making it a promising material for use as an HTM in PSCs. Poly(3-hexylthiophene) (P3HT), on the other hand, is a semiconducting polymer with good film-forming properties and high hole mobility, and a relatively higher LUMO (lowest unoccupied molecular orbital) energy level than that of NiPc.[11] Unfortunately, single NiPc and P3HT may not be optimal HTMs for PSCs in terms of energy levels to achieve good efficiencies. In general, the energy levels of the HTM should be such that the HOMO (highest occupied molecular orbital) level is higher than the valence band maximum (VBM) of the perovskite, and the LUMO level is higher than the conduction band minimum of the perovskite, in order to facilitate efficient charge transfer and minimize recombination losses at the interface. By the way, the use of a bilayered HTM in PSCs can provide several advantages. First, the different properties of the two materials can complement each other and improve the overall performance of the device. Second, due to the possibility of a cascade energy level in the bilayer structure, the recombination of charge carriers occurring at the interface between the HTM and the perovskite layer can be minimized, ultimately leading to improved efficiency of the device. Finally, the use of two materials with high stability can improve the long-term stability of the device. In this regard, combining NiPc and P3HT into a bilayered HTM can potentially improve the stability and efficiency of PSCs.
Different techniques are available for forming a bilayered HTM in PSCs. One of these involves depositing the NiPc layer prior to the P3HT layer, while another utilizes phase separation from a mixed solution of NiPc and P3HT during coating. Sequential deposition can have an impact on the performance of a bilayer HTM, as the second deposition may cause damage to the first deposition, ultimately affecting the shape and properties of the material. To ensure optimal outcomes, it is crucial to form a bilayer through spontaneous phase separation,[12] which involves applying a single coating.
In this study, a bilayer of NiPc and P3HT was developed for use as a hole transport layer (HTL) in PSCs. During the coating process, the mixed solution was phase-separated to form two layers, with the NiPc layer as the lower layer and the P3HT layer as the upper layer. The mixing ratio of the two materials was found to be critical for the performance of the PSCs, and the best results were achieved at a weight ratio of 1:0.3 to NiPc:P3HT, exhibiting a high power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $23.11\%$ , which is a significant improvement over PSCs with single-layer HTLs. Furthermore, the PSCs exhibited good operational stability, with more than $90\%$ of the initial efficiency maintained for $300\mathrm{{h}}$ under illumination conditions. The polyisobutylene (PIB)-encapsulated PSCs maintained more than $90\%$ of the initial PCE for $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ under $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $85\%$ relative humidity (RH).
## 2. Result and Discussion
The NiPc complex was synthesized by reacting Ni with a metalfree phthalocyanine (Pc) center,[10] as described in the Experimental Section. Compared to metal-free Pc, the synthesized NiPc demonstrated greater thermal stability, as confirmed by thermogravimetric analysis and differential scanning calorimetry in Figure S1 in the Supporting Information. Addition of nickel resulted in an increase in the glass transition temperature of NiPc from 201.17 to $284.35^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , further enhancing its thermal stability as shown in Figure S1b in the Supporting Information. The energy band level of synthesized NiPc was determined using cyclic voltammetry (CV), with the HOMO level being found to be $-5.26\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Figure S2, Supporting Information). The LUMO was calculated by adding the HOMO value to $1.9\ \mathrm{eV},$ which is the band gap of NiPc determined by UV-vis absorption spectroscopy, as shown in Figure S3 in the Supporting Information. Figure 1a displays the energy levels of formamidinium lead tri-iodide $({\mathrm{FAPbI}}_{3}) $ ) perovskite and NiPc, alongside poly(methyl methacrylate) (PMMA), P3HT, and spiro-OMeTAD for the purpose of comparison. As expected, the LUMO level of NiPc and the conduction band minimum of perovskite exhibit a small difference, which is insufficient to effectively block the crossover of photoelectrons generated in the perovskite to the HTL. This insufficient electron blocking can significantly reduce the fill factor (FF) of PSCs due to the low shunt resistance, thereby limiting their efficiency. As illustrated in Figure S4 and Table S1 in the Supporting Information, this FF limitation can be mitigated by introducing a thin interlayer of PMMA with a wide bandgap. The PMMA interlayer successfully improves electron blocking, compensating for the low LUMO of the HTL, as we have demonstrated previously.[13] While PMMA with a wide bandgap can still be effective in low LUMO P3HT, its impact is weaker compared to NiPc, as demonstrated in the comparison. Interestingly, the addition of PMMA resulted in a slight improvement in efficiency even in spiro-OMeTAD with sufficient LUMO level (Figure S5, Supporting Information). This effect is believed to be attributed by the perovskite surface passivation of PMMA.[13,14] Despite the improvement in efficiency achieved by combining NiPc and PMMA, the resulting efficiency is still lower compared to the conventional spiro-OMeTAD. Due to the planar molecular structure of Pc,[15] it exhibits relatively low conductivity in the stacking direction, and it is preferable to apply it as a very thin film. However, forming a uniform thin film with such a small thickness is challenging, and as a result, improving efficiency is difficult due to charge leakage through the thin film. Hybridizing the NiPc with polymeric P3HT is a potential solution to further improve the performance of PSCs. The hybridization process involves mixing P3HT and NiPc in a solution. In structures such as PSCs, where multiple layers are stacked,[16] the alignment of energy levels between each layer is critical. This is because the charges generated in the absorption layer need to be efficiently directed in the desired direction. Therefore, if the mixed NiPc and P3HT can form a vertically bilayer configuration as depicted in Figure 1a, it has the potential to be highly efficient in transferring photogenerated holes. There will be several ways to fabricate a bilayered HTM in PSCs. One approach is to deposit the NiPc layer first, followed by the P3HT layer. The use of a solution-based sequential coating process can potentially cause the dissolution or damage of previously coated layers during subsequent coating stages. This may significantly impact the morphology and properties of the bilayered HTM, consequently influencing the overall performance of the device. Therefore, the best alternative is to induce spontaneous bilayer separation during coating of a mixed solution of NiPc and P3HT. This separation could be caused by the difference in surface energy between NiPc and P3HT, which led us to explore the surface energy. Figure S6 in the Supporting Information displays contact angle images of NiPc and P3HT with deionized (DI) water, with measured contact angles of $98.44^{\circ}$ for NiPc and $106.36^{\circ}$ for P3HT, indicating that P3HT is more hydrophobic. Using these results, we calculated the contact angles and surface energies of NiPc and P3HT with eight samples. In Figure 1b, NiPc exhibits a contact angle of $98.37\pm1.25^{\circ}$ and a calculated surface energy of $24.04\pm$ $0.76\mathrm{mN}\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ . In contrast, P3HT has a contact angle of $106.21\pm$ $0.67^{\circ}$ and a surface energy of $19.29\pm0.41~\mathrm{mN}~\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ . Therefore, due to its hydrophobic properties and low surface energy, P3HT is expected to be preferentially distributed at the air-side surface region. Consequently, we anticipate that phase separation will occur during the spin-coating process because the two materials have different surface energies.[17]
To confirm this, we employed time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (TOF-SIMS) to investigate the distribution of NiPc and P3HT in the HTL (Figure S7, Supporting Information).
As shown in Figure $\mathtt{S7a}$ in the Supporting Information, the $\mathrm{Ni^{+}}$ peak with the NiPc film was found to be uniform in the HTL region, suggesting that Ni ions were evenly distributed. After the addition of P3HT, a new $S^{+}$ peak emerged, which was identified as P3HT. This caused the $\mathrm{Ni^{+}}$ peak to be partially separated, and the ${\mathsf{S}}^{+}$ peak appeared between the $\mathrm{Ni^{+}}$ peaks, as seen in Figure $\mathbf{S}7\mathbf{b}$ in the Supporting Information. Furthermore, the ${\sf S}^{+}$ peak tends to increase after a slight decrease on the surface. To verify the distribution difference between P3HT and NiPc, we measured the change in intensity ratio between [S] and [Ni] in Figure S8 in the Supporting Information. The surface of the HTL had a relatively high proportion of sulfur ions, indicating that the mixed NiPc and P3HT were partially separated within the HTL. P3HT, which was distributed abundantly on the surface region, has an advantage for charge transport within the HTL due to favorable energy band matching with NiPc. To investigate the effect of the mixed NiPc and P3HT on the surface region, we employed ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS). In Figure 1c, NiPc has a work function (WF) of $4.83\ \mathrm{eV},$ which differs from the VBM by $0.47\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ indicating that the HOMO level of NiPc measured by UPS is $\approx-5.3\mathrm{eV}.$ This value is consistent with the HOMO energy level measured by CV as discussed earlier. In contrast, the WF of mixed NiPc and P3HT was found to be $4.69\mathrm{eV}$ with a difference of $0.5\mathrm{eV}$ to the VBM, leading to a calculated HOMO energy level of $\approx-5.19\ \mathrm{eV}.$ Therefore, the mixed NiPc and P3HT layer increases the HOMO energy level of the HTL surface by $0.11\mathrm{eV},$ highlighting the effect of P3HT on the surface region. To clarify the electrical properties and chemical states of the partially separated HTL on the surface region, we investigated the interaction between elements using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements. The absence of the $\mathtt{S2p}$ peak in the NiPc film and its presence in the mixed HTL with P3HT suggest that P3HT is present on the surface region of the HTL, as depicted in Figure S9a in the Supporting Information. The XPS results of C 1s, N 1s, and Ni 2p in NiPc and P3HT-mixed HTL are presented in Figure S9b–d in the Supporting Information. Under the P3HT-mixed condition, the binding energy of C 1s and N 1s is measured at a lower position, while the binding energy peak of Ni 2p remains almost unchanged. To gain further insight into electrical potential distribution of the bilayered HTL, we performed cross-sectional Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) in dark and under illumination in open circuit condition as shown in Figure S10 in the Supporting Information. The contact potential difference (CPD) can be calculated from the difference in WF of the sample surface and the tip. The WF of the sample surface can be obtained by measuring CPD using the tip with a known WF. The CPD images from cross-sectional KPFM for NiPc and P3HT-mixed NiPc are shown in Figure S10a in the Supporting Information. The CPD line profiles across the layers in dark and under illumination for pure NiPc and mixed NiPc and P3HT-based devices are shown in Figure $\mathrm{S10b,c}$ in the Supporting Information, while the fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) layers were grounded. The surface photovoltage (SPV) was then calculated by subtracting the CPD measured under illumination from the CPD obtained in dark condition as represented in Figure 1d. Under the illumination, electrons are separated and move toward the electron transporting layer (ETL, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ) and while the holes are transported to the HTL. Therefore, SPV is lower at the ETL due to accumulation of electrons and SPV gradually increases toward the HTL as responsible from hole accumulation. The mixed NiPc and P3HT device demonstrates the higher SPV in the HTL region, which implies more effective charge separation and a higher accumulation of holes, leading to a lower SPV in the perovskite active layer when compared to the pure NiPc device. Conversely, the SPV for the pure NiPc device peaked at the interface between the perovskite layer and the HTL, and subsequently decreased in the HTL region, which indicates a hindrance in the transportation of holes from the perovskite layer, resulting in a higher SPV in the perovskite layer. Due to the nonuniform thickness of each layer in the perovskite device and the localized nature of cross-sectional KPFM measurements, the CPD values of each layer may vary slightly between samples. Consequently, our focus lies on comparing the relative difference in CPDs obtained under illumination and in the dark, rather than solely considering the absolute WF values of each layer. This approach allows us to assess the variations in CPDs and their significance in understanding the charge accumulation behavior at the interface.
Subsequently, we were able to deduce the electric field difference (EFD) by taking the first derivative of CPD for further investigation in dark condition as shown in Figure S11a,b in the Supporting Information. The variation of EFD in both ETL and HTL regions of a mixed NiPc and P3HT device is completely opposite to each other. This implies a balanced charge distribution at each interface, i.e., between the ETL/perovskite and perovskite/HTL, resulting in a nearly gradual EFD line profile from ETL to HTL, compared to that of pure NiPc device. This electric field distribution within the perovskite layer is beneficial to the charge carrier transport under the illumination which is evident from the SPV profile for the mixed NiPc and P3HT device. Notably, the EFD in the HTL region of the mixed NiPc and P3HT device is about four times larger than that of the pure NiPc device (Figure S11c, Supporting Information). This enhancement is attributed to the formation of a bilayer structure consisting of NiPc and P3HT, as well as the WF mismatch between perovskite and HTL, effectively establishing built-in potential at the perovskite/HTL interface, which facilitates the hole transport and leads to enhanced hole accumulation under illumination, compared to the pure NiPc device.
In the formation of a bilayer by phase separation of NiPc and P3HT, the optimal mixing ratio of the two materials is important for optimizing both electron blocking and hole transport. The effect of the mixing ratio on hole transport was investigated, and photoluminescence (PL) was used to measure the quality of the perovskite film and the ability of the charge-transporting layer. Figure 2a shows the steady-state PL measured after coating a solution of NiPc and P3HT mixed at weight ratios of 0.1, 0.3, and 0.5 on the perovskite layer. The PL intensity decreased as the mixing ratio of P3HT to NiPc increased to 0.1 and 0.3, but increased when the mixing ratio increased to 0.5. The timeresolved PL (TRPL) exhibited the same tendency of decay time as the steady-state PL (Figure 2b). A decay time of 275.2 ns was measured under the HTL-free condition in Table S2 in the Supporting Information, and the decay times dependent on the mixing ratio of P3HT to NiPc were calculated to be 185.4, 150.8, 112.3, and 173.4 ns, respectively, with the shortest decay time observed at a mixing ratio of NiPc:P3HT of 1:0.3. This suggests that the charge generated from the perovskite can be extracted most efficiently at this ratio, as evidenced by the lowest PL intensity and shortest decay time. To further investigate the effect of mixing ratio on charge transfer, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was performed at a bias of $0.9\mathrm{~V~}$ under dark conditions. The results show a difference in charge transfer depending on the mixing ratio of NiPc:P3HT. Figure 2c depicts Nyquist $\left(Z^{\prime}–Z^{\prime\prime}\right)$ plots obtained from EIS measurements conducted on fully fabricated devices, which were coated with mixed solutions consisting of varying ratios of P3HT to NiPc. The plot exhibits a semicircular shape that represents a parallel RC circuit as a function of frequency. At high frequencies, the capacitor acts as a short circuit and causes $Z$ to approach the origin. Conversely, at low frequencies, the capacitor’s impedance becomes infinite according to Equation (1)
$$
Z_{\mathrm{c}}=\mathrm{\Omega}-\frac{j}{\omega C}=\mathrm{\Omega}-\frac{j}{2\pi f C}
$$
As shown in Figure 2c, as the mixing ratio of P3HT to NiPc increases, both the value of the right $x$ -intercept and radius in the semicircle increase in comparison to the single NiPc condition. The maximum values are reached at a mixing ratio of 1:0.3. The larger size of the semicircle corresponds to a higher recombination resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)$ , which leads to a decrease in the recombination at the interface.[18] Moreover, in Figure S12 in the Supporting Information, when zooming in on the origin, which could represent a series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{S}}\right)$ in the high-frequency region, the $Z$ value of P3HT-mixed conditions reduces compared to the condition without P3HT. The increased recombination resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\right)$ observed from the EIS measurement can increase the open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ of the devices, while the reduced $R_{\mathrm{{S}}}$ at the high-frequency region can improve the FF,[19] ultimately leading to the enhancement of the performance of the PSCs. To investigate the further effects of P3HT-mixed NiPc, the morphology of the HTL was analyzed using scanning electron microscopy (SEM). The surface image of NiPc without P3HT in Figure 2d reveals cracks at the grain boundaries region, whereas the surface of mixed NiPc and P3HT shows better morphology with no cracks, regardless of the mixing amount of P3HT used (1:0.3– 0.5). Although the morphology of perovskite has been widely studied, the surface morphology of the HTL, which affects the interface between HTL and the electrode, has received relatively little attention. The SEM results indicate that cracks on the HTL surface can be infiltrated by Au particles during the thermal evaporation process, leading to direct contact with the perovskite and reducing the device’s performance. This could potentially have a negative impact on the device’s overall performance. Improving the morphology of the HTL by mixing it with P3HT can protect against charge leakage from the perovskite layer. To confirm the potential for enhanced device performance, we used KPFM to measure the electric field difference under illumination conditions, as previously described. Our results (Figure S13, Supporting Information) show that the mixed NiPc and P3HT-based devices exhibit superior charge separation and extraction compared to NiPc-based devices during PSC operation.
Combining these advantages, we expected that forming vertical bilayers by mixing P3HT into NiPc would have multiple positive effects on the production of high-efficiency PSCs. For the fabrication of these PSCs, we deposited a compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{c}-\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)}$ ) and a nanostructured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer $\mathrm{(ns–TiO_{2})}$ with a thickness of ${\approx}150~\mathrm{nm}$ as the ETL. This was followed by a perovskite layer about $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ thickness, coated with an ultra-thin passivation layer, and about $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick NiPc/P3HT HTL on an FTO substrate. These major layers can be clearly observed in the cross-sectional SEM image (Figure S14, Supporting Information). Figure 3a shows the statistical distribution of the PCE of 25 PSCs fabricated under identical conditions using bilayer HTMs with NiPc and P3HT mixed in different weight ratios. The PCE tended to increase as the weight ratio of P3HT mixed into NiPc increased to 0.1 and 0.3. However, when the weight ratio reached 0.5, the PCE decreased, indicating that the maximum occurred at a weight ratio of 0.3. The maximum PCE was achieved with a weight ratio of 0.3 because this ratio led to a simultaneous increase in the three parameters that determine PCE: current density $(J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}})$ , FF, and $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ , as shown in Figure S15 in the Supporting Information. The PCE results are in agreement with the predictions based on the previous PL and EIS measurements. Specifically, the bilayer HTL formed using the optimal weight ratio of NiPc and P3HT showed the most efficient charge extraction and the lowest rate of charge recombination. The $J{-}V$ characteristics of the best-performing device in reverse and forward bias sweeps with antireflective coatings on the device surface are illustrated in Figure 3b. The $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF values calculated from the $J{-}V$ curve at a reverse scan mode were $26.04~\mathrm{mA~cm^{-2}}$ , $1.11~\mathrm{V},$ and $80.11\%$ , respectively, with the PCE of $23.11\%$ under standard air mass 1.5 conditions, with a $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ , $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ , FF, and PCE of $26.05\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , $1.09\mathrm{V},$ $76.53\%$ , and $21.68\%$ , respectively, at the forward bias. The external quantum efficiency (EQE) provides information on the mechanisms of photon absorption, exciton generation, and charge transport of separated carriers of PSCs. Figure 3c shows the EQE for the same device as that of $J{-}V.$ The $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ obtained by integrating EQE over the wavelength is $24.6\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . The EQE of the control and target PSC is similar to NiPc and P3HTblended HTL-based device. However, intensity of EQE slightly increased around $500\mathrm{nm}$ region of the blended HTL-based device. We measured UV-vis absorption to confirm additional light absorption of control and target HTL in Figure S16 in the Supporting Information. New optical absorption peaks were measured after P3HT blending in the wavelength around $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ where the intensity of EQE increased regardless of film and solution. It means that P3HT can also act as a light absorber while acting as a hole conductor, thus $J_{\scriptscriptstyle\mathrm{SC}}$ is increased after P3HT blending.[20]
PSCs require long-term stability, with a particular emphasis on thermal stability. Three stability tests were conducted, including thermal stability, operational stability, and accelerated longterm stability. The thermal stability test was performed by measuring the normalized PCE of the device at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $40\%$ RH without encapsulation, which maintained about $80\%$ of its initial efficiency for ${\approx}500$ h (Figure 4a). The operational stability was evaluated using an encapsulated device without a UV cut-off filter, which maintained above $90\%$ efficiency for $300\mathrm{h}$ (Figure 4b). For accelerated long-term stability, the device was encapsulated with PIB and kept at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $85\%$ RH. Figure S17 in the Supporting Information shows a time-varying photograph of the device maintained under these conditions. The target device exhibited almost the same efficiency as its initial performance for
$700\mathrm{~h~}$ , and even after $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ , it retained $\approx90\%$ of its initial efficiency (Figure 4c). This is evident from the comparison of the $J{-}V$ curves before $(18.20\%)$ and after $(16.29\%)$ the $1000\mathrm{h}$ stability test (Figure S18, Supporting Information).
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, our study demonstrates that the use of a bilayer HTL composed of NiPc and P3HT can significantly improve the efficiency and stability of PSCs. The optimization of the mixing ratio between NiPc and P3HT, as well as the coating process, is critical for achieving the desired phase separation and vertical bilayer formation, which enhances the charge extraction and electron blocking from the perovskite layer to the HTL. Our results indicate that the optimal amount of P3HT in the mixed HTL leads to a PCE of over $23\%$ , the highest reported for Pcbased PSCs, while exceeding the optimal amount reduces the PCE slightly. The phase-separated HTL also exhibits reduced decay time of TRPL and increased recombination resistance at the perovskite–HTL interface, further improving PSC performance. Moreover, we found that the operational stability of the PSCs is maintained above $90\%$ for $300\mathrm{~h~}$ under illumination conditions. Encapsulation with PIB further enhances the stability of the PSCs, making them suitable for practical applications. Specifically, the PIB-encapsulated PSC maintains about $90\%$ of its initial PCE even under harsh conditions of $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $85\%$ RH for $1000\mathrm{h}$ . Overall, our findings provide insights into the design and optimization of bilayer HTLs for high-efficiency and stable PSCs, which have great potential for commercial and industrial applications in the future.
## 4. Experimental Section
Synthesis of Formamidinium Lead Iodide: Formamidinium iodide (FAI) was synthesized by reacting $20\mathrm{g}$ of formamidine acetate $99\%$ , Alfa Aesar) and $30~\mathsf{m L}$ of hydroiodic acid $57\%$ in water, Aldrich) in a $250~\mathrm{mL}$ roundbottom flask at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ temperature and 1 mbar pressure for $\textsf{l h}$ with rotation. The resulting product was then dissolved in ethanol, recrystallized from diethyl ether, and finally dried at room temperature (RT) in a vacuum oven for $24\ h$ . Methylamine hydrochloride (MACl) was dissolved in ethanol, recrystallized from diethyl ether, and dried at RT in a vacuum oven for $24\mathsf{h}$ . Formamidinium lead iodide $(\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3})$ was synthesized by reacting formamidinium iodide (FAI), lead(II) iodide, $99.9985\%$ (metal basis, Alfa Aesar), and $77m L$ of 2-methoxyethanol (anhydrous $99.8\%$ , Aldrich) in a $70~\mathrm{mL}$ vial with stirring. The mixed solution was heated up to ${\mathsf{120}}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and then precipitated using the retrograde method. The resulting $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ black powder was filtered and baked at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ .
Synthetic Route of Nickel Phthalocyanine: The synthesis of nickel phthalocyanine was conducted using the method reported by Yu et al.[10] In a $200~\mathrm{mL}$ two-necked flask, 2,9,16,23-tetra-tert-butyl- ${\cdot}29H,37H.$ phthalocyanine $(200\mathrm{~mg},\ 0.27\mathrm{~mmol})$ ) and nickel acetate tetrahydrate $(400~\mathsf{m g}$ , $\mathsf{1.64}\mathsf{m m o l}$ ) were mixed. Next, 1-pentanol $(45~\mathsf{m L})$ and tripropylamine $(687~\upmu\up L)$ were added into the flask under an argon atmosphere. The reaction mixture was heated to reflux at $160^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ under an argon atmosphere. The reaction completion was monitored using thinlayer chromatography. After cooling to RT, the reaction mixture was extracted with ethyl acetate and brine water. The solvent was then distilled off under reduced pressure. The resulting residue was purified by column chromatography using silica gel and dichloromethane/hexane (200:300, $\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v})$ as the eluent. The blue fraction was collected and concentrated to afford a blue solid, NiPc, in $65\%$ yield $(740~\mathsf{m g})$ . The matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization-time of flight $m/z$ values were calculated for $\mathsf{C}_{48}\mathsf{H}_{48}\mathsf{N}_{8}\mathsf{N i}$ to be 795.66 and found to be 795.54.
Materials: 2,9,16,23-Tetra-tert-butyl-29H,31H-phthalocyanine and nickel acetate, 1-pentanol, and tripropylamine were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich Co. LLC. Column chromatography and thin layer chromatography (TLC) were performed on silica gel 60 (230-400 mesh, Merch), while silica gel (60) F254 plates (Merck) were used for
TLC. All purchased chemicals were used as received without further purification.
Solar Cell Fabrication: FTO was etched using zinc powder (Aldrich) and hydrochloric acid $(57\mathrm{~wt\%~}$ in ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ , Aldrich). The substrate was then sonicated in a sequence of detergent, DI water, and ethanol. Next, a thin compact $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ layer was deposited onto an FTO (Asahi) substrate using a spray coater equipment with titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetylacetonate) in ethanol (1:20 volume ratio) at $450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ An ETL, nanostructured (ns)- $\cdot\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ was spin-coated at $1500~\mathsf{r p m}$ onto the substrate using commercial $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ paste (SC-HT040, Sharechem) diluted with 2-methoxyethanol and terpinol (3.5:1 weight ratio), and then heat treated at $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $\textsf{l h}$ in ambient air to remove the organic components. $\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ ( $(7.26~\mathsf{M})$ with $3.8\ m\mathrm{ol}\%$ of methylenediammonium chloride $(\mathsf{M D A C l}_{2}$ ) and $35\ m\circ\%$ methylammonium hydrochloride (MACl) was dissolved in a mixed solvent of dimethylformamide and dimethyl sulfoxide (8:1 volume ratio) at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ The resulting solution was coated onto the $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ layer by a two-step spin-coating process, with a first step at 1000 for $\rceil0\ s$ , followed by a second step at 5000 rpm for $20\thinspace s$ During the second step, the substrate was treated with drop-casting of diethyl ether. The substrate was dried on a hot plate at 150 $^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for 15 min and then at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $15\mathrm{\min}$ to produce $(\mathsf{F A P b l}_{3}$ with 3.8 $m o l\%$ of ${\mathsf{M D A C l}}_{2}$ ) mixed halide perovskite thin film. The heat-treated film was coated with $0.75{\mathrm{~mg}}$ methyl methacrylate polymer (PMMA, TCI) in 1 mL chlorobenzene solution at 5000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ and then dried at RT. PMMA was dissolved in $7m L$ of chlorobenzene at $50^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathsf{h}$ with stirring. The prepared film was dried at RT in a vacuum oven for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . For the preparation of HTL, $\boldsymbol{{\mathrm{10~mg}}}$ of synthesized nickel(II) 2,9,16,23-tetratert-butyl-29H,31H-phthalocyanine (NiPc) and varying weight ratios (1, 3, $\textsf{5m g}$ of P3HT (Nano Clean Tech) were mixed in $7m L$ of chlorobenzene along with organic additives. The organic additives were consisted of $7.5~\upmu\up L$ of Li bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (Li-TFSI)/acetonitrile $(770\:m\mathrm{g/1}\:m L)$ ) and $7.5\upmu\up L$ of tBP/acetonitrile (1:1 volume ratio). This mixture was used to create a $N i\mathsf{P c}\colon\mathsf{P3H T}$ mixed solution. Subsequently, the solution was spin-coated onto the $\mathsf{F T O}/\mathsf{c}.\mathsf{T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{S n O}_{2}/\mathsf{P M M A}/$ perovskite layer at a speed of 3000 rpm for $30\mathrm{{s}}$ . During the coating process, the different surface energies of NiPc and $\mathsf{P3H T}$ caused them to spontaneously separate, thereby forming a bilayer HTL. Finally, an Au counter electrode was deposited through thermal evaporation.
Characterization: The $J-V$ characteristics of the devices were measured using a Keithley 2400 source meter with a step voltage of $\boldsymbol{{\mathsf{10}}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{m}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{V}}$ and a delay time of $40~\mathsf{m s}$ , under $100\ m\times\mathsf{w}\ c m^{-2}$ (AM 1.5G) illumination by a solar simulator (Newport, Oriel Sol3A Class AAA) and a calibrated Si reference cell certified by NREL. The active area was controlled using a mask with an aperture of $0.094~{\mathsf{c m}}^{2}$ . The morphology of the films was measured using SEM (Nova NanoSEM), and their crystallinity was determined using X-ray diffraction (D/MAX2500V/PC). The optical properties of the films were characterized using UV-vis spectroscopy (Jasco V-780), while XPS and UPS were conducted using ESCALAB 250 XI. TOF-SIMS was also performed using TOF-SIMS 5 (ION TOF). The hydrophobicity of the perovskite films was measured using contact angle equipment (DSA100, KRUSS). EIS was conducted using Metrohm Autolab (AUT302N). The steady-state PL and TRPL spectra were measured using a commercial time-correlated single photon counting setup (FluoTime 300, PicoQuant GmbH) equipped with a PMA-C-192-M detector and high-resolution excitation monochromators. EQE was determined using an internal quantum efficiency system (Oriel, IQE 200B) under irradiation by a 100 W Xenon lamp.
